=== How to use ===
1. In the Teams developer portal open Tools, API key registration, create a key, add the secret, set the base URL to the servers entry, choose the target tenant and app, and copy the registration ID.
2. Paste the registration ID into reference_id. If you use the Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit with an existing OpenAPI file, it registers the key and updates the manifest for you.
3. Create a dedicated, read-only API key for Copilot so that you can rotate or revoke it without touching other consumers.
4. Add the image host of imageUrl to validDomains of the app manifest so that thumbnails are shown.
5. Test with an unknown SKU and with an empty search result and make sure the API returns clear 404 or empty responses.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Using ApiKeyPluginVault for an MCP server. MCP plugins do not support API key authentication; use OAuth 2.0 there.
- Leaving securitySchemes out of the OpenAPI file, so the toolkit and Copilot cannot tell where to place the key.
- Registering a base URL that does not match servers, which makes the key unusable at run time.
- Embedding the key value in the manifest, the OpenAPI file or the agent instructions.
- Sharing one powerful key between Copilot and back-office systems; a leak then exposes everything.

