=== How to use ===
1. Adapt site, list name and the columns Status, DueDate and AssignedTo (person column). Choice column filters use the text value.
2. Create an environment variable PmoMailbox and turn the flow on with a service account that can read the list.
3. Run it manually and check the owner list in the Unique_owners output.
4. Optionally add a second digest for the manager level by grouping on a department column.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Calling Get items once per owner. That multiplies requests; one query plus Filter array is faster and avoids throttling.
- Forgetting pagination. Without the threshold only 100 items are read by default.
- Dividing ticks incorrectly. One day equals 864000000000 ticks (100-nanosecond units).
- Using high concurrency for mails. Five parallel iterations are enough and keep you below mail limits.
- Mailing people who left the company. Check the account status or use a group in test mode first.

=== Testing ===
- Set one task overdue for two different people and run the flow. Each person gets only their own table.
- Complete all tasks and check that the run ends with Succeeded and no mail is sent.
- Check the day count against a date calculation by hand.

App.OnStart with twenty Set() calls is slow, order-dependent and hard to maintain. Named formulas (App.Formulas) are evaluated lazily and always up to date, and user-defined functions give you one place for logic you would otherwise copy into ten controls.

## Formulas (App > Formulas property)

```powerfx
// ---- design tokens ----
cBrand = RGBA(0, 90, 158, 1);
cDanger = ColorValue("#B00020");
cMuted = RGBA(96, 94, 92, 1);

// ---- current user, resolved once and cached ----
curUser = LookUp(Employees, Email = Lower(User().Email));
isManager = !IsBlank(curUser) && curUser.Role.Value = "Manager";

// ---- thresholds in one place ----
maxAttachmentMB = 10;
dueSoonDays = 3;

// ---- user-defined functions ----
FormatMoney(amount: Number): Text = Text(amount, "[$-en-US]$#,##0.00");

DaysOverdue(due: Date): Number = Max(0, DateDiff(due, Today(), TimeUnit.Days));

IsDueSoon(due: Date): Boolean =
    due >= Today() && DateDiff(Today(), due, TimeUnit.Days) <= dueSoonDays;

IsValidEmail(s: Text): Boolean = IsMatch(Trim(s), Match.Email);

StatusColor(s: Text): Color =
    Switch(s, "Open", cBrand, "Overdue", cDanger, cMuted);

InitialsOf(fullName: Text): Text =
    Upper(Left(fullName, 1) & Mid(fullName, Find(" ", fullName & " ") + 1, 1));
```

## Using them in controls

```powerfx
// lblAmount.Text
FormatMoney(ThisItem.Amount)

// lblStatus.Color
StatusColor(ThisItem.Status.Value)

// icoWarning.Visible
ThisItem.Status.Value <> "Done" && DaysOverdue(ThisItem.DueDate) > 0

// btnApprove.DisplayMode
If(isManager, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)

// txtEmail.BorderColor
If(IsValidEmail(txtEmail.Text) || IsBlank(txtEmail.Text), cMuted, cDanger)
```

## How to use

1. Open App, choose the Formulas property and paste the block. Every definition ends with a semicolon.
2. Replace the Employees table and Role column with your own.
3. Delete the matching Set(...) lines from App.OnStart. What stays in OnStart is only what truly must run once and has side effects, such as LoadData.
4. User-defined functions have been generally available in canvas apps since September 2025 (the preview switch was removed in Studio version 2508.3). If the formula bar still rejects the function syntax, update the app to the current Studio version and check that the new analysis engine setting under Settings > Updates is on (it is on by default for new apps). In either case, confirm the formulas in the formula bar before you rely on them.

## Common mistakes

- Treating a named formula like a variable. You cannot assign to it with Set, and it re-evaluates when its dependencies change. That is the benefit, but do not use it for values that must stay frozen, such as the time a button was clicked.
- Forgetting parameter types. Every user-defined function parameter needs a type (name: Type) and the function needs a return type.
- Putting behavior (Patch, Navigate, Notify) in a plain function. Functions defined with = are pure. Behavior functions need curly braces and the return type Void and are only valid where behavior formulas are allowed.
- Circular references, for example cBrand defined using a function that uses cBrand. The editor flags it, fix by splitting the definition.
- Heavy data calls inside a function used in a gallery template. They are evaluated for every row.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- curUser = LookUp(Employees, Email = Lower(User().Email)) is delegable on Dataverse and SharePoint when Email is a text column; Lower on the right side is a constant and is fine.
- Named formulas are evaluated on demand, which makes the first screen render faster than a long OnStart.
- Function and type names (Number, Text, Boolean, Date, DateTime, Color) follow Power Fx; Boolean is the type name for true/false. The examples use only simple types; passing records and tables through user-defined types was rolled out later than functions, so check the current status before relying on it.
- Named formulas cannot be changed at runtime; for values the user edits keep using Set or UpdateContext.

Validation that only runs on submit is annoying; validation that shouts on first load is worse. This pattern shows messages after the user touched a field and keeps the submit button disabled until everything passes.

## Field rules (define once, reuse everywhere)

Paste each rule inline into the properties that use it, or centralize them as named formulas if your app accepts control references in App.Formulas (confirm in the formula bar). The rule names below are used by the later snippets.

```powerfx
chkTitle = Len(Trim(txtTitle.Text)) >= 3;
chkEmail = IsMatch(Trim(txtEmail.Text), Match.Email);
chkPhone = IsBlank(txtPhone.Text) || IsMatch(txtPhone.Text, "^\+?[0-9 ()\-]{7,20}$");
chkAmount = IfError(Value(txtAmount.Text), 0) > 0 && IfError(Value(txtAmount.Text), 0) <= 10000;
chkDue = !IsBlank(dpDue.SelectedDate) && dpDue.SelectedDate >= Today();
allValid = chkTitle && chkEmail && chkPhone && chkAmount && chkDue;
```

## Error labels (Label.Text and Label.Visible)

```powerfx
// lblTitleError.Visible
varTouchedTitle && !chkTitle

// lblTitleError.Text
"Enter at least 3 characters."

// lblAmountError.Text
If(
    !IsNumeric(txtAmount.Text),
    "Enter a number.",
    "Amount must be between 0.01 and 10,000."
)
```

Mark a field as touched once the user has edited it:

```powerfx
// txtTitle.OnChange   (and OnSelect of the submit button for all fields)
Set(varTouchedTitle, true)

// Submit button: show every message once on a failed attempt
// btnSubmit.OnSelect
If(
    !allValid,
    Set(varTouchedTitle, true);
    Set(varTouchedEmail, true);
    Set(varTouchedAmount, true);
    Notify("Please fix the highlighted fields.", NotificationType.Warning),
    SubmitForm(frmRequest)
)
```

Border color feedback:

```powerfx
// txtEmail.BorderColor
If(varTouchedEmail && !chkEmail, ColorValue("#B00020"), RGBA(138, 136, 134, 1))
```

## With an Edit form control

```powerfx
// frmRequest.OnSuccess
Notify("Request saved", NotificationType.Success);
ResetForm(frmRequest);
Navigate(scrList)

// frmRequest.OnFailure
Notify("Save failed: " & frmRequest.Error, NotificationType.Error)

// DataCard "Title".Required  -> true
// btnSubmit.DisplayMode
If(frmRequest.Valid, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)
```

## How to use

1. Rename controls to match, or edit the names in the chk formulas.
2. Add one error label per field, placed under the input.
3. Reset the touched variables in Screen.OnVisible: Set(varTouchedTitle, false) and so on.
4. Use frmRequest.Valid when you rely on the Form control and its required and length settings; use the chk formulas when you build the layout yourself.

## Common mistakes

- Calling Value() on text that is not a number. Value("abc") yields an error that propagates through And(); wrap it in IfError or test IsNumeric first.
- Regex without anchors. IsMatch(text, "\d{5}") is true if five digits appear anywhere. Use ^...$ or MatchOptions.Complete.
- Using an email regex of your own. Match.Email is built in and maintained.
- Validating only in the app. Add required columns, ranges and length limits in Dataverse or SharePoint as well, because the app is not the only way in.
- Comparing dates with text. dpDue.SelectedDate is a Date value; Today() is also a Date.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- Validation formulas run on the client only and involve no delegation.
- IsMatch uses .NET-style regular expressions; the pattern must be a constant (a literal or a named formula that does not depend on a control), otherwise the editor reports an error.
- Match.Email, Match.Digit and MatchOptions.Complete are predefined enumerations.

Field workers lose coverage. Canvas apps can keep working if you cache reads and queue writes in local collections stored with SaveData.

## Startup (App.OnStart)

```powerfx
If(
    Connection.Connected,
    ClearCollect(colTasks, Filter(Tasks, AssignedTo.Email = User().Email));
    SaveData(colTasks, "tasksCache"),
    LoadData(colTasks, "tasksCache", true)
);
LoadData(colPending, "pendingEdits", true)
```

The third argument true means "do not raise an error if the file does not exist yet" which is what you want on the first launch.

## Save an edit locally first (btnSave.OnSelect)

```powerfx
Collect(
    colPending,
    {
        LocalId: GUID(),
        TaskId: varTask.ID,
        NewStatus: ddStatus.Selected.Value,
        Note: txtNote.Text,
        EditedAt: Now()
    }
);
SaveData(colPending, "pendingEdits");
// reflect the change in the cached list so the UI updates immediately
Patch(colTasks, LookUp(colTasks, ID = varTask.ID), {Status: {Value: ddStatus.Selected.Value}});
SaveData(colTasks, "tasksCache");
Notify(If(Connection.Connected, "Saved, syncing...", "Saved offline, will sync later"), NotificationType.Information)
```

## Sync the queue (Timer.OnTimerEnd, Duration 30000, Repeat true, AutoStart true)

```powerfx
If(
    Connection.Connected && !IsEmpty(colPending) && !varSyncing,
    Set(varSyncing, true);
    // iterate over a snapshot, because rows are removed from colPending inside the loop
    ClearCollect(colBatch, colPending);
    ForAll(
        colBatch As P,
        // true/false keep both branches the same type, because the IfError result ends up in the ForAll output table
        IfError(
            Patch(Tasks, LookUp(Tasks, ID = P.TaskId), {Status: {Value: P.NewStatus}, Notes: P.Note});
            Remove(colPending, LookUp(colPending, LocalId = P.LocalId));
            true,
            // keep the row in the queue, try again next tick
            Collect(colSyncErrors, {LocalId: P.LocalId, Message: FirstError.Message});
            false
        )
    );
    SaveData(colPending, "pendingEdits");
    Set(varSyncing, false)
)
```

Status indicator for the header:

```powerfx
// lblSync.Text
If(
    !Connection.Connected, "Offline - " & CountRows(colPending) & " pending",
    CountRows(colPending) > 0, "Syncing " & CountRows(colPending) & "...",
    "All changes saved"
)
```

## How to use

1. Publish and test in the Power Apps mobile app: switch to airplane mode, edit, reconnect and watch the queue drain.
2. Show colSyncErrors on an admin or diagnostics screen so failed rows can be inspected.
3. Add a "Sync now" button with the same logic as the timer, for impatient users.

## Common mistakes

- Testing only in Power Apps Studio. SaveData and LoadData behave differently there and in the browser player; the device storage is the reliable target.
- Storing sensitive data. The local file is not a secure vault. Cache only what the user may keep on the device.
- Modifying a collection while iterating it in ForAll. The sync formula loops over the snapshot colBatch and removes rows from colPending by the stable LocalId key.
- No conflict strategy. Two people editing the same row offline produce last-writer-wins. Compare Modified with EditedAt before patching if that matters.
- Letting the cache grow without bound. Filter at load time and clear old rows.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- Filter with AssignedTo.Email: confirm there is no delegation warning for your connector; the data cached by ClearCollect is capped by the data row limit (500 by default).
- Connection.Connected, SaveData, LoadData and GUID are available in canvas apps; SaveData storage is per app and per device.
- For heavy offline needs with relational data, use the Dataverse mobile offline profile for model-driven or canvas apps instead of hand-rolled queues.

// ==== What it does ====
// Finds source IP addresses that produce many failed interactive sign-ins and classifies them:
//   - PasswordSpray: many distinct users, few attempts per user
//   - BruteForce: few users, many attempts per user
//   - Mixed/other: everything above the volume threshold that fits neither pattern
// Error codes that only mean 'the user must complete an extra step' (MFA prompt, KMSI prompt) are excluded,
// because they are not real authentication failures and create most of the noise in this kind of query.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// SigninLogs: TimeGenerated, UserPrincipalName, IPAddress, ResultType (string), ResultDescription,
//   AppDisplayName, ClientAppUsed, LocationDetails (dynamic), IsInteractive.
// Workspace: Log Analytics workspace that receives the Entra ID 'SignInLogs' diagnostic category.

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback        = 24h;   // time window to analyse
let minFailures     = 20;    // minimum failed attempts per IP to be listed
let sprayMinUsers   = 8;     // distinct users from one IP that signals spraying
let sprayMaxPerUser = 5;     // average attempts per user must stay at or below this
// Result codes that are not credential failures but 'interrupts' (MFA required, KMSI, etc.)
let interruptCodes  = dynamic(["0", "50058", "50072", "50074", "50076", "50079", "50125", "50140"]);
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where IsInteractive == true
| where ResultType !in (interruptCodes)
| extend Country = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion)
| summarize
    FailedAttempts = count(),
    DistinctUsers  = dcount(UserPrincipalName),
    DistinctApps   = dcount(AppDisplayName),
    Users          = make_set(UserPrincipalName, 25),
    Errors         = make_set(ResultType, 10),
    Countries      = make_set(Country, 5),
    FirstSeen      = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen       = max(TimeGenerated)
    by IPAddress
| where FailedAttempts >= minFailures
| extend AttemptsPerUser = round(todouble(FailedAttempts) / DistinctUsers, 1)
| extend Pattern = case(
    DistinctUsers >= sprayMinUsers and AttemptsPerUser <= sprayMaxPerUser, "PasswordSpray",
    DistinctUsers <= 3 and AttemptsPerUser > sprayMaxPerUser,              "BruteForce",
    "Mixed")
| extend ActiveMinutes = datetime_diff("minute", LastSeen, FirstSeen)
| project IPAddress, Pattern, FailedAttempts, DistinctUsers, AttemptsPerUser, ActiveMinutes,
          Countries, Errors, FirstSeen, LastSeen, Users
| order by Pattern asc, FailedAttempts desc

// ---- Companion query: users that were hit hardest, with the final outcome ----
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where IsInteractive == true
| summarize
    Failures  = countif(ResultType !in ("0", "50058", "50072", "50074", "50076", "50079", "50125", "50140")),
    Successes = countif(ResultType == "0"),
    IPs       = dcount(IPAddress),
    LastEvent = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserPrincipalName
| where Failures >= 10
// A success after many failures from many IPs is the pattern that deserves an immediate look.
| extend SuspiciousSuccess = Successes > 0 and IPs >= 3
| order by SuspiciousSuccess desc, Failures desc

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Lower minFailures for small tenants; raise it if one NAT gateway of a large office shows up.
// Add 'and ResultType in ("50126", "50053")' to focus on wrong password (50126) and locked account (50053).
// Join the result against a watchlist of corporate egress IPs (_GetWatchlist('CorpIPs')) and drop known ranges.
// To alert in Microsoft Sentinel, save the first query as an analytics rule, run every 1h over 1d,
//   and map IPAddress to the IP entity and Users to the Account entity.
// Repeat the logic on AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs for token-based spray against legacy clients.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Filter on TimeGenerated first and keep the summarize by one compact key (IPAddress).
// make_set with a size limit (25, 10, 5) keeps result rows small and avoids memory pressure.
// dcount is approximate (HyperLogLog); that is fine for thresholds and much cheaper than exact counts.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Comparing ResultType to a number: in SigninLogs the column is a string, so use "0" and not 0.
// Forgetting interrupt codes: MFA prompts (50074, 50076) are not failures and inflate every count.
// Using UserPrincipalName for the key only: guest users show up with #EXT# names, normalise if needed.
// Treating a high count as proof of attack: CGNAT and misconfigured mail clients also produce bursts.

// ==== What it does ====
// Looks at successful sign-ins, orders them per user by time and uses prev() to compare consecutive events.
// The great-circle distance comes from geo_distance_2points(), the speed is distance divided by elapsed time.
// Rows are returned when the implied speed is above a threshold (default 900 km/h, roughly an airliner),
// the countries differ and the IP addresses differ. VPN exits and mobile carriers are the main false positives.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// SigninLogs: TimeGenerated, UserPrincipalName, IPAddress, ResultType, AppDisplayName,
//   LocationDetails (dynamic: city, countryOrRegion, geoCoordinates.latitude / longitude).

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback       = 3d;
let minDistanceKm  = 500;    // ignore short hops
let minSpeedKmh    = 900;    // faster than this is considered impossible
let ignoredApps    = dynamic(["__none__"]);   // e.g. dynamic(["Microsoft Intune Enrollment"])
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResultType == "0"
| where AppDisplayName !in (ignoredApps)
| extend City    = tostring(LocationDetails.city),
         Country = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion),
         Lat     = todouble(LocationDetails.geoCoordinates.latitude),
         Lon     = todouble(LocationDetails.geoCoordinates.longitude)
| where isnotempty(Country) and isnotnull(Lat) and isnotnull(Lon)
| project TimeGenerated, UserPrincipalName, IPAddress, City, Country, Lat, Lon, AppDisplayName
// prev() needs a serialized (sorted) row set
| sort by UserPrincipalName asc, TimeGenerated asc
| serialize
| extend PrevUser    = prev(UserPrincipalName),
         PrevTime    = prev(TimeGenerated),
         PrevIP      = prev(IPAddress),
         PrevCity    = prev(City),
         PrevCountry = prev(Country),
         PrevLat     = prev(Lat),
         PrevLon     = prev(Lon),
         PrevApp     = prev(AppDisplayName)
| where UserPrincipalName == PrevUser
| where IPAddress != PrevIP and Country != PrevCountry
// geo_distance_2points(lon1, lat1, lon2, lat2) returns METERS - note longitude comes first
| extend DistanceKm = round(geo_distance_2points(PrevLon, PrevLat, Lon, Lat) / 1000.0, 0)
| extend Hours      = datetime_diff("second", TimeGenerated, PrevTime) / 3600.0
| where DistanceKm >= minDistanceKm
// guard against division by zero when two sign-ins carry the same timestamp
| extend SpeedKmh = round(DistanceKm / max_of(Hours, 0.01), 0)
| where SpeedKmh >= minSpeedKmh
| project UserPrincipalName,
          FromTime = PrevTime, FromCity = PrevCity, FromCountry = PrevCountry, FromIP = PrevIP, FromApp = PrevApp,
          ToTime = TimeGenerated, ToCity = City, ToCountry = Country, ToIP = IPAddress, ToApp = AppDisplayName,
          DistanceKm, Hours = round(Hours, 2), SpeedKmh
| order by SpeedKmh desc

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Tune minSpeedKmh (lower values such as 500 also catch fast ground travel but add noise, 1000 or more reduces noise) and minDistanceKm.
// Exclude trusted VPN or SASE egress ranges: where not(ipv4_is_in_range(IPAddress, '203.0.113.0/24')).
// Join to IdentityInfo or a watchlist to suppress shared service mailboxes or travelling executives.
// Add a second pass that requires different DeviceDetail.deviceId values to reduce false positives further.
// Entra ID Protection already raises an 'atypical travel' risk detection; use this as a transparent cross-check.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Keep lookback short; sort and serialize run on the full projected row set in memory.
// Project only the needed columns before sort to reduce the data that has to be ordered.
// A 3-day window over a large tenant is fine; for 30 days, partition with 'partition by UserPrincipalName'.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Swapping longitude and latitude in geo_distance_2points: the function expects longitude first.
// Forgetting serialize: prev() raises an error or returns wrong rows on unsorted data.
// Comparing only events with different cities: city names change within one trip, compare countries.
// Ignoring that mobile network egress can geolocate to another country than the phone.

// ==== What it does ====
// Successful sign-ins whose AuthenticationRequirement is singleFactorAuthentication did not require MFA.
// The query groups them by user and by application, so you can see where a Conditional Access policy is missing,
// scoped too narrowly or excluded. It also reports how many of those sign-ins came from outside known countries.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// SigninLogs: TimeGenerated, UserPrincipalName, AppDisplayName, ResourceDisplayName, ResultType,
//   AuthenticationRequirement, ConditionalAccessStatus, ClientAppUsed, IPAddress, LocationDetails, IsInteractive.

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback = 30d;
// Resources that legitimately never prompt for MFA in your tenant (review and extend)
let excludedResources = dynamic(["Microsoft Intune Enrollment"]);
// Countries where you expect users; everything else is flagged
let homeCountries = dynamic(["DE", "AT", "CH", "US"]);
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
let SingleFactor =
    SigninLogs
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where ResultType == "0"
    | where IsInteractive == true
    | where AuthenticationRequirement == "singleFactorAuthentication"
    | where ResourceDisplayName !in (excludedResources)
    | extend Country = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion);
// 1) Users with the most single-factor sign-ins and the apps they used
// Part 1 and the let block above belong together; parts 2 and 3 stand alone
SingleFactor
| summarize
    SingleFactorSignIns = count(),
    Apps                = make_set(AppDisplayName, 10),
    ClientApps          = make_set(ClientAppUsed, 5),
    CAResults           = make_set(ConditionalAccessStatus, 5),
    OutsideHome         = countif(Country !in (homeCountries) and isnotempty(Country)),
    LastSeen            = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserPrincipalName
| order by SingleFactorSignIns desc
| take 100

// 2) Applications ranked by the share of single-factor sign-ins
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ResultType == "0" and IsInteractive == true
| summarize
    Total        = count(),
    SingleFactor = countif(AuthenticationRequirement == "singleFactorAuthentication"),
    Users        = dcount(UserPrincipalName)
    by AppDisplayName
| extend SingleFactorPct = round(100.0 * SingleFactor / Total, 1)
| where SingleFactor > 0
| order by SingleFactor desc

// 3) Legacy authentication, which cannot do MFA at all
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ResultType == "0"
| where ClientAppUsed !in ("Browser", "Mobile Apps and Desktop clients")
| summarize Sessions = count(), Users = dcount(UserPrincipalName) by ClientAppUsed, AppDisplayName
| order by Sessions desc

// ==== How to adapt ====
// The let block is only shared by part 1; parts 2 and 3 are standalone queries.
// To test a planned policy, filter ConditionalAccessStatus == 'notApplied' and look at who would be hit.
// Use report-only results: expand ConditionalAccessPolicies and look for result 'reportOnlyFailure'.
// Add a join to IdentityInfo to exclude disabled accounts and break-glass accounts by name.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Single-factor sign-ins can be very frequent, so summarize early and use take after ordering only.
// countif inside one summarize is faster than several separate filtered queries joined later.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Treating every single-factor row as a gap: a trusted-location or compliant-device policy can legitimately skip MFA.
// Using ConditionalAccessStatus alone: it only says whether a policy applied, not which control was granted.
// Looking only at interactive sign-ins and ignoring AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs for token refreshes.
// Putting two separate result sets in one saved analytics rule; a rule must return one table.

// ==== What it does ====
// Attackers who gain Application Administrator rights often add a client secret to an existing service principal and use it silently.
// Part 1 lists credential additions from AuditLogs with actor and target. Part 2 compares recent service principal
// sign-ins with a 30-day baseline of IP addresses and highlights those from IPs that never appeared before.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// AuditLogs: TimeGenerated, OperationName, Result, InitiatedBy (dynamic), TargetResources (dynamic), CorrelationId.
// AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs: TimeGenerated, ServicePrincipalId, ServicePrincipalName, AppId, IPAddress, ResultType, ResourceDisplayName.
// Both tables need the matching Entra ID diagnostic categories (AuditLogs, ServicePrincipalSignInLogs).

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Part 1: credentials added to apps or service principals --------------
AuditLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where OperationName has_any ("Add service principal credentials",
                               "Certificates and secrets management")
| where Result == "success"
| extend Actor      = coalesce(tostring(InitiatedBy.user.userPrincipalName), tostring(InitiatedBy.app.displayName)),
         ActorIP    = tostring(InitiatedBy.user.ipAddress),
         TargetName = tostring(TargetResources[0].displayName),
         TargetId   = tostring(TargetResources[0].id),
         TargetType = tostring(TargetResources[0].type)
| project TimeGenerated, OperationName, Actor, ActorIP, TargetName, TargetType, TargetId, CorrelationId
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// ---- Part 2: service principal sign-ins from IPs not seen in the baseline ---
let baselineStart = ago(30d);
let recentStart   = ago(2d);
let KnownIPs =
    AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs
    | where TimeGenerated >= baselineStart and TimeGenerated < recentStart
    | where ResultType == "0"
    | summarize by ServicePrincipalId, IPAddress;
AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs
| where TimeGenerated >= recentStart
| where ResultType == "0"
| summarize
    SignIns   = count(),
    Resources = make_set(ResourceDisplayName, 5),
    FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen  = max(TimeGenerated)
    by ServicePrincipalId, ServicePrincipalName, AppId, IPAddress
// leftanti keeps only rows that have NO match in the baseline
| join kind=leftanti KnownIPs on ServicePrincipalId, IPAddress
| order by SignIns desc

// ---- Part 3 (optional): correlate - sign-ins of the same app within 24 hours after a credential change ----
let CredChanges =
    AuditLogs
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
    | where OperationName has_any ("Add service principal credentials", "Certificates and secrets management")
    | extend TargetName = tostring(TargetResources[0].displayName), ChangeTime = TimeGenerated
    | project ChangeTime, TargetName, Actor = tostring(InitiatedBy.user.userPrincipalName);
AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) and ResultType == "0"
| join kind=inner CredChanges on $left.ServicePrincipalName == $right.TargetName
| where TimeGenerated between (ChangeTime .. ChangeTime + 24h)
| summarize SignIns = count(), IPs = make_set(IPAddress, 10) by ServicePrincipalName, Actor, ChangeTime

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Add 'Add owner to application' and 'Add app role assignment to service principal' to the has_any list of Part 1 to also see ownership and permission changes.
// Exclude automation: where Actor !in ('deployment-pipeline-app') or where ActorIP !startswith '10.'.
// For Microsoft Sentinel rules, wrap Part 1 alone and map Actor to Account and ActorIP to IP entities.
// Include managed identities with the AADManagedIdentitySignInLogs table if you use them.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Narrow OperationName first; AuditLogs is large and has_any on a few phrases is cheap.
// join kind=leftanti with a small summarized right side is efficient; always summarize the baseline first.
// Keep baseline and recent windows disjoint (>= start and < boundary versus >= boundary) so a sign-in cannot count as both baseline and recent.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Matching OperationName with == : the portal uses an en dash in 'Update application - Certificates...'; use has_any.
// Assuming InitiatedBy.user always exists: app-initiated changes put the name in InitiatedBy.app.
// Using a baseline that is too short: rarely used automation looks new every time.
// Forgetting that ServicePrincipalSignInLogs must be enabled separately in the diagnostic setting.

// ==== What it does ====
// Runs against the 'resources' table of Azure Resource Graph (not Log Analytics).
// It builds a list of missing tag names per resource, skips types that cannot carry tags, and offers a summary
// per subscription so owners can be chased. Use it before assigning an Azure Policy 'require tag' effect with deny.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// Azure Resource Graph tables: resources (name, type, resourceGroup, subscriptionId, location, tags),
//   resourcecontainers (type 'microsoft.resources/subscriptions', name, subscriptionId).
// Run in the Azure portal (Resource Graph Explorer), Azure CLI 'az graph query' or Search-AzGraph.

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Detail list: every resource with at least one missing tag --------------
resources
// resource types that do not support tags or are noise
| where type !in~ ("microsoft.network/networkwatchers",
                   "microsoft.network/networkwatchers/flowlogs",
                   "microsoft.compute/virtualmachines/extensions",
                   "microsoft.alertsmanagement/smartdetectoralertrules")
| extend Owner       = tostring(tags['Owner']),
         CostCenter  = tostring(tags['CostCenter']),
         Environment = tostring(tags['Environment'])
| extend MissingOwner  = isempty(Owner),
         MissingCC     = isempty(CostCenter),
         MissingEnv    = isempty(Environment)
| where MissingOwner or MissingCC or MissingEnv
| extend MissingTags = strcat(
    iff(MissingOwner, "Owner ", ""),
    iff(MissingCC,    "CostCenter ", ""),
    iff(MissingEnv,   "Environment", ""))
| join kind=leftouter (
    resourcecontainers
    | where type == "microsoft.resources/subscriptions"
    | project subscriptionId, SubscriptionName = name
  ) on subscriptionId
| project SubscriptionName, resourceGroup, name, type, location, MissingTags, tags
| order by SubscriptionName asc, resourceGroup asc, type asc

// ---- Summary per subscription (run as a separate query) -----------------------
resources
| where type !in~ ("microsoft.network/networkwatchers", "microsoft.compute/virtualmachines/extensions")
| extend HasAll = isnotempty(tostring(tags['Owner']))
                  and isnotempty(tostring(tags['CostCenter']))
                  and isnotempty(tostring(tags['Environment']))
| summarize Total = count(), Compliant = countif(HasAll), NonCompliant = countif(not(HasAll)) by subscriptionId
| extend CompliancePct = round(100.0 * Compliant / Total, 1)
| join kind=leftouter (
    resourcecontainers
    | where type == "microsoft.resources/subscriptions"
    | project subscriptionId, SubscriptionName = name
  ) on subscriptionId
| project SubscriptionName, Total, Compliant, NonCompliant, CompliancePct
| order by NonCompliant desc

// ---- Resource groups (tags on the container itself) ----------------------------
resourcecontainers
| where type == "microsoft.resources/subscriptions/resourcegroups"
| where isempty(tostring(tags['Owner']))
| project subscriptionId, resourceGroup = name, location

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Replace the three tag names with your own standard; add more extend/iff pairs for further tags.
// Remove the type exclusions if you want every single resource, or extend them for types you cannot tag.
// Export with 'az graph query -q @query.kql --first 1000' and page using --skip for larger tenants.
// Use the same logic as Azure Policy 'Require a tag on resources' once the cleanup is done.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Resource Graph is fast, but each call returns at most 1000 rows (the default and the maximum of --first / $top); page with --skip, which is ignored when the query itself uses take or limit.
// Only the join kinds innerunique, inner, leftouter and fullouter are supported, with at most three join or union operations per query.
// Prefer filtering on type early; avoid parse and regex on all rows.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Azure treats tag names as case-insensitive, but tags['Owner'] in the query is case-sensitive: a resource tagged 'owner' is reported as missing Owner.
// Reading tags with tags.Owner when the key contains spaces or hyphens: use the bracket form tags['Cost-Center'].
// Expecting resources inherit tags from the resource group: they do not unless a policy copies them.
// Running this text in Log Analytics: the resources table only exists in Resource Graph.

// ==== What it does ====
// Every Azure Monitor Agent or Log Analytics agent sends a heartbeat roughly once per minute.
// Part 1 shows computers whose last heartbeat is older than a threshold. Part 2 computes the availability
// (percentage of minutes with a heartbeat) so you can differentiate between dead machines and unstable connections.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// Heartbeat: TimeGenerated, Computer, Category, OSType, OSName, ComputerEnvironment,
//   ResourceGroup, SubscriptionId, ResourceId, Version, ComputerIP.

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Part 1: machines that went silent --------------------------------------
let silentAfter  = 15m;    // no heartbeat for this long counts as silent
let forgetAfter  = 14d;    // machines silent longer than this are probably decommissioned
Heartbeat
| where TimeGenerated > ago(forgetAfter)
| summarize
    LastHeartbeat = max(TimeGenerated),
    Category      = take_any(Category),
    OSType        = take_any(OSType),
    ResourceGroup = take_any(ResourceGroup)
    by Computer
| extend SilentFor = now() - LastHeartbeat
| where SilentFor > silentAfter
| extend Status = case(SilentFor < 1h,  "Just stopped",
                       SilentFor < 24h, "Down today",
                       "Down for days")
| project Computer, OSType, Category, ResourceGroup, LastHeartbeat, SilentFor, Status
| order by LastHeartbeat desc

// ---- Part 2: availability of the last 24 hours --------------------------------
let windowStart = ago(24h);
let totalMinutes = 24 * 60;
Heartbeat
| where TimeGenerated > windowStart
// one row per computer and minute first, then count the minutes: exact, unlike the approximate dcount()
| summarize by Computer, Minute = bin(TimeGenerated, 1m)
| summarize MinutesWithHeartbeat = count() by Computer
| extend AvailabilityPct = round(min_of(100.0, 100.0 * MinutesWithHeartbeat / totalMinutes), 2)
| where AvailabilityPct < 99
| order by AvailabilityPct asc

// ---- Part 3: chart of heartbeat count per 15 minutes for the worst machines ----
let Worst =
    Heartbeat
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
    | summarize Beats = count() by Computer
    | top 5 by Beats asc
    | project Computer;
Heartbeat
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Computer in (Worst)
| summarize Beats = count() by Computer, bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)
| render timechart

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Change silentAfter to 5m for critical servers; for scheduled-shutdown machines raise it or tag them out.
// Filter on ResourceGroup or ComputerEnvironment == 'Azure' to separate Azure VMs from Arc or on-premises servers.
// In Azure Monitor, create a log search alert on Part 1 with 'Number of results greater than 0'.
// Combine with the resources table in Resource Graph to detect VMs that have never reported at all.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Heartbeat is small and cheap, but the distinct-minute count over many machines still scales with computers x minutes.
// Use take_any instead of max for columns that are constant per computer.
// Restrict the time range to what the alert needs; a 14-day scan is for reports, not for 5-minute alert rules.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Computing availability with count()/1440: duplicate heartbeats in one minute inflate the result; reduce to one row per bin(TimeGenerated, 1m) first.
// Treating every silent machine as an incident: deallocated dev VMs are normal.
// Reading a low availability for a machine onboarded today: it did not exist for the whole 24 hours.
// Forgetting that an alert on absence needs the data to exist in the lookback window at all.
// Mixing Computer name casing from different agents; use tolower(Computer) when joining to other tables.

// ==== What it does ====
// Reads performance counters collected by the agent into the Perf table and ranks machines by sustained load.
// Percentiles are far more informative than averages for capacity planning: a server that averages 30 percent CPU
// can still sit at 95 percent for an hour every night. The query handles Windows and Linux counter names.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// Perf: TimeGenerated, Computer, ObjectName, CounterName, InstanceName, CounterValue.
// Counters must be enabled in the Data Collection Rule (for example '\Processor Information(_Total)\% Processor Time'
//   and '\Memory\% Committed Bytes In Use' on Windows, 'Processor' and 'Memory' objects on Linux).

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback    = 7d;
let hotLevel    = 90.0;   // percent that counts as 'hot'
let minSamples  = 50;     // ignore machines with too little data
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Statements 2, 3 and 4 all use the 'Normalised' let block below: copy that let block in front of the statement you run,
//   because a let is not shared between separate runs.
// 1) Normalise CPU and memory counters from Windows (AMA and MMA) and Linux into one shape
let Normalised =
    Perf
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where (ObjectName in ("Processor", "Processor Information") and CounterName == "% Processor Time" and InstanceName == "_Total")
         or (ObjectName == "Memory" and CounterName in ("% Committed Bytes In Use", "% Used Memory"))
    | extend Metric = iff(CounterName == "% Processor Time", "CPU", "Memory")
    | project TimeGenerated, Computer, Metric, Value = CounterValue;
// 2) Per computer and metric statistics
Normalised
| summarize
    Samples = count(),
    Avg     = round(avg(Value), 1),
    P50     = round(percentile(Value, 50), 1),
    P95     = round(percentile(Value, 95), 1),
    P99     = round(percentile(Value, 99), 1),
    Max     = round(max(Value), 1),
    HotPct  = round(100.0 * countif(Value >= hotLevel) / count(), 1)
    by Computer, Metric
| where Samples >= minSamples
| order by P95 desc
| take 50

// 3) Wide view: one row per computer with CPU and memory side by side
Normalised
| summarize P95 = round(percentile(Value, 95), 1) by Computer, Metric
| summarize
    CpuP95 = maxif(P95, Metric == "CPU"),
    MemP95 = maxif(P95, Metric == "Memory")
    by Computer
| extend Verdict = case(
    CpuP95 >= 90 and MemP95 >= 90, "Undersized",
    CpuP95 >= 90,                  "CPU bound",
    MemP95 >= 90,                  "Memory bound",
    CpuP95 < 15 and MemP95 < 40,   "Oversized - candidate for resize",
    "OK")
| order by Verdict asc, CpuP95 desc

// 4) Chart: hourly average CPU of the five busiest machines
let Top5 =
    Normalised
    | where Metric == "CPU"
    | summarize AvgCpu = avg(Value) by Computer
    | top 5 by AvgCpu desc
    | project Computer;
Normalised
| where Metric == "CPU" and Computer in (Top5)
| summarize AvgCpu = avg(Value) by Computer, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
| render timechart

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Add '% Free Space' (LogicalDisk) or 'Avg. Disk sec/Transfer' counters in the same pattern for disk pressure.
// Lower hotLevel to 80 for latency-sensitive workloads; raise lookback to 30d for right-sizing exercises.
// Filter on Computer startswith 'sql' or a resource group via a join to Heartbeat to scope to one team.
// Windows '% Committed Bytes In Use' is the commit ratio (virtual memory), not physical RAM use; add 'Available MBytes' for physical pressure.
// Linux memory: '% Used Memory' counts caches, use '% Available Memory' with inverted logic if you prefer.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// percentile is more expensive than avg; keep the time range and computer set tight.
// Define the normalisation once in a let statement and reference it several times inside one query; for separate runs copy the let in front of each statement.
// If Perf volume is large, consider reducing the DCR sample frequency from 10 s to 60 s.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Averaging averages: CounterValue is already a sample; always aggregate raw samples.
// Mixing Processor and Processor Information instances: filter InstanceName == '_Total' or you count every core.
// Judging 'oversized' from one week that excludes month-end peaks.
// Reading the Verdict column without checking sample counts: a machine with a few samples gives unstable percentiles.

// ==== What it does ====
// After a mailbox takeover attackers create inbox rules that forward mail to an external address, delete replies,
// or move messages containing words like 'invoice' or 'security' into obscure folders. This query reads the
// Exchange audit events (New-InboxRule and Set-InboxRule from the Exchange admin audit log), turns the Parameters array into columns
// and scores each rule so the worst ones sort to the top.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// OfficeActivity (Microsoft 365 connector in Sentinel): TimeGenerated, OfficeWorkload, Operation, UserId,
//   ClientIP, Parameters (JSON array of Name/Value pairs), OfficeObjectId, ResultStatus.
// Mailbox auditing must be on (default for Microsoft 365, verify with Get-OrganizationConfig).

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback = 14d;
let ruleOps  = dynamic(["New-InboxRule", "Set-InboxRule"]);
let hideFolders = dynamic(["RSS Feeds", "RSS Subscriptions", "Conversation History", "Archive", "Junk Email", "Deleted Items"]);
let keywords = dynamic(["invoice", "payment", "wire", "bank", "password", "security", "phish", "hacked", "iban", "urgent"]);
let corpDomain = "contoso.com";   // your own mail domain, used to detect external targets
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OfficeActivity
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where OfficeWorkload == "Exchange" and Operation in~ (ruleOps)
| where ResultStatus in~ ("True", "Succeeded", "Success", "PartiallySucceeded")
// Parameters is an array of {Name, Value}; turn it into one property bag per row
| extend Params = todynamic(Parameters)
| mv-apply p = Params on (
    summarize Bag = make_bag(bag_pack(tostring(p.Name), tostring(p.Value))))
| extend RuleName    = coalesce(tostring(Bag.Name), OfficeObjectId),
         ForwardTo   = coalesce(tostring(Bag.ForwardTo), tostring(Bag.RedirectTo), tostring(Bag.ForwardAsAttachmentTo)),
         DeleteMsg   = tostring(Bag.DeleteMessage) =~ "True",
         MoveTo      = tostring(Bag.MoveToFolder),
         SubjectKeys = strcat(tostring(Bag.SubjectContainsWords), " ", tostring(Bag.SubjectOrBodyContainsWords), " ", tostring(Bag.BodyContainsWords)),
         MarkRead    = tostring(Bag.MarkAsRead) =~ "True"
| extend ExternalForward = isnotempty(ForwardTo) and not(ForwardTo has corpDomain),
         HidesMail       = DeleteMsg or MoveTo has_any (hideFolders),
         KeywordHit      = SubjectKeys has_any (keywords)
// simple additive risk score
| extend Score = iff(ExternalForward, 5, 0) + iff(isnotempty(ForwardTo), 2, 0)
                + iff(HidesMail, 2, 0) + iff(KeywordHit, 2, 0) + iff(MarkRead, 1, 0)
| where Score >= 3
| project TimeGenerated, UserId, Operation, RuleName, ForwardTo, DeleteMsg, MoveTo, MarkRead,
          SubjectKeys, ClientIP, Score, ExternalForward, HidesMail, KeywordHit
| order by Score desc, TimeGenerated desc

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Replace contoso.com with your accepted domains; add a list of partner domains that may receive forwards.
// 'has corpDomain' is a simple check: a recipient list that mixes internal and external addresses counts as internal, so tighten it with a regex if needed.
// Correlate ClientIP with SigninLogs for the same user in the hour before the rule was created.
// Add the 'Set-Mailbox' operation with ForwardingSmtpAddress or ForwardingAddress parameters for mailbox-level forwarding.
// Pair with the Defender XDR table CloudAppEvents (ActionType 'New-InboxRule') if you use Defender for Cloud Apps.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Filter OfficeWorkload and Operation before parsing JSON; mv-apply on all Exchange events would be very costly.
// make_bag over bag_pack is cheap per row, but keep the lookback moderate (14 to 30 days).
// Score thresholds are applied after parsing, so keep the pre-filter as narrow as possible.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Expecting Parameters to be a ready-made object: it is an array of name/value pairs and needs mv-apply.
// Comparing booleans with == true: values arrive as the string 'True', hence the =~ comparison.
// Rules created in Outlook clients are logged as UpdateInboxRules (mailbox audit) with the rule details in OperationProperties, not Parameters; this query does not parse them, so cover them with a second query.
// Alerting on every forward: legitimate out-of-office or shared-mailbox rules exist; keep the score threshold.

// ==== What it does ====
// Encoded PowerShell (-EncodedCommand and its abbreviations) is used by admins and by attackers alike.
// This hunt extracts the Base64 string from the command line, decodes it, removes the UTF-16 null bytes and
// searches the decoded script for suspicious building blocks such as IEX, DownloadString and AMSI tampering.
// Results list one row per execution, ordered by risk; the overview query at the end summarises per parent process to spot outliers.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// DeviceProcessEvents (Defender XDR / Microsoft Defender for Endpoint): Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, AccountName,
//   AccountDomain, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
//   InitiatingProcessParentFileName, ProcessIntegrityLevel, SHA256, ReportId.
// Run in security.microsoft.com under Hunting > Advanced hunting, or in Sentinel when the Defender XDR connector is on (there, replace Timestamp with TimeGenerated).

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback = 7d;
// Parents that commonly run encoded commands for good reasons (extend after review)
let knownParents = dynamic(["ccmexec.exe", "healthservice.exe", "monitoringhost.exe"]);
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(lookback)
| where FileName in~ ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "powershell_ise.exe")
// -e, -ec, -en, -enc ... -encodedcommand (all valid abbreviations) followed by a long Base64 string.
// The pattern deliberately does not match -ExecutionPolicy, which also starts with -e.
| where ProcessCommandLine matches regex @"(?i)\s-e(c|n[a-z]*)?\s+[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{20,}"
| extend EncodedPart = extract(@"(?i)\s-e(?:c|n[a-z]*)?\s+([A-Za-z0-9+/=]{20,})", 1, ProcessCommandLine)
// PowerShell encodes in UTF-16LE: strip non-printable characters after decoding
| extend Decoded = replace_regex(base64_decode_tostring(EncodedPart), @"[^\x20-\x7E]", "")
| where isnotempty(Decoded)
// count suspicious building blocks; the single capture group is required by extract_all
| extend TokenHits = array_length(extract_all(
        @"(?i)(?:^|[\s;|(.:])(iex|invoke-expression|downloadstring|downloadfile|net\.webclient|frombase64string|-nop|-noprofile|-w hidden|-windowstyle hidden|amsiutils|invoke-mimikatz|reflection\.assembly|invoke-webrequest|start-bitstransfer|add-mppreference)\b",
        Decoded))
| extend HasUrl = Decoded matches regex @"(?i)https?://"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ (knownParents)
| extend Risk = case(TokenHits >= 2 or (TokenHits >= 1 and HasUrl), "High",
                     TokenHits == 1 or HasUrl,                     "Medium",
                     "Low")
| extend RiskRank = case(Risk == "High", 1, Risk == "Medium", 2, 3)
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountDomain, AccountName, Risk, RiskRank, TokenHits, HasUrl,
          Parent = InitiatingProcessFileName, GrandParent = InitiatingProcessParentFileName,
          ParentCmd = InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
          Decoded = substring(Decoded, 0, 400), SHA256, ReportId
// sort by the rank: sorting the text 'Risk' would give High, Low, Medium
| order by RiskRank asc, Timestamp desc
| project-away RiskRank

// ---- Overview: who launches encoded PowerShell most often? --------------------
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "powershell.exe" and ProcessCommandLine matches regex @"(?i)\s-e(c|n[a-z]*)?\s+[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{20,}"
| summarize Runs = count(), Devices = dcount(DeviceId), Accounts = dcount(AccountName)
    by InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by Runs desc

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Add every legitimate management tool to knownParents after you verified it; the overview query shows candidates.
// Raise the Base64 length to 40 to skip short test strings, or lower it to 12 to catch tiny stagers.
// Turn it into a custom detection rule: add ReportId, DeviceId and Timestamp to the output and schedule hourly.
// Include 'cmd.exe /c powershell' chains by also checking InitiatingProcessCommandLine.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Filter by FileName and Timestamp first; the regex and decoding only run on the reduced row set.
// Advanced Hunting limits results to 10,000 rows and 30 days of data; keep lookback within that.
// Avoid decoding inside a where over all rows; extend after the cheap filters, as done here.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Decoding without stripping NUL bytes: base64_decode_tostring on UTF-16 text gives 'p o w e r s h e l l' with gaps.
// Matching only '-EncodedCommand': attackers use -e, -ec or -enc. The regex covers abbreviations.
// Using Timestamp in Log Analytics tables: the Sentinel copy of this data uses TimeGenerated.
// Treating Low risk as safe: short encoded stagers often have no readable keywords.

// ==== What it does ====
// Aggregates failed logons per source IP, target account and machine, then joins to successful logons that follow
// within a configurable window. The result lists accounts that got in after repeated failures and shows
// how long the guessing lasted and how many failures preceded the success.

// ==== Required tables/columns ====
// SecurityEvent: TimeGenerated, EventID, Computer, TargetUserName, TargetDomainName, IpAddress, LogonType,
//   Status, SubStatus, WorkstationName, LogonProcessName, AuthenticationPackageName.
// Collected by the Windows Security Events connector (AMA); the 'Common' or 'All events' set contains 4624 and 4625.

// ==== Query ====
// The statements below are independent parts: run them one at a time unless stated otherwise.
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let lookback       = 1d;
let failThreshold  = 10;     // failed logons before we care
let successWindow  = 30m;    // success must follow the last failure within this time
let remoteLogons   = dynamic([3, 10]);      // 3 = network, 10 = RemoteInteractive (RDP)
let ignoredIPs     = dynamic(["-", "::1", "127.0.0.1", ""]);
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
let Failures =
    SecurityEvent
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where EventID == 4625
    | where LogonType in (remoteLogons)
    | where IpAddress !in (ignoredIPs)
    | where TargetUserName !endswith "$"          // skip machine accounts
    | summarize
        FailCount = count(),
        FirstFail = min(TimeGenerated),
        LastFail  = max(TimeGenerated),
        SubStatuses = make_set(SubStatus, 5)
        by Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName, TargetDomainName
    | where FailCount >= failThreshold;
let Successes =
    SecurityEvent
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where EventID == 4624
    | where LogonType in (remoteLogons)
    | where IpAddress !in (ignoredIPs)
    | project SuccessTime = TimeGenerated, Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName, TargetDomainName, LogonType, LogonProcessName;
Failures
| join kind=inner Successes on Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName, TargetDomainName
// the success must come after the last failure and within the window
| where SuccessTime >= LastFail and SuccessTime <= LastFail + successWindow
| extend GuessingMinutes = datetime_diff("minute", LastFail, FirstFail)
| summarize
    FirstSuccess = min(SuccessTime),
    SuccessLogons = count(),
    SubStatuses   = take_any(SubStatuses)
    by Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName, TargetDomainName, FailCount, FirstFail, LastFail,
       GuessingMinutes, LogonType
| project Computer, IpAddress, Account = strcat(TargetDomainName, "\\", TargetUserName),
          LogonType, FailCount, GuessingMinutes, FirstFail, LastFail, FirstSuccess, SuccessLogons, SubStatuses
| order by FailCount desc

// ---- Reference: common SubStatus values for 4625 (SecurityEvent stores them as lowercase hex strings) ----
//   0xc000006a  wrong password for a valid user
//   0xc0000064  user name does not exist
//   0xc0000234  account locked out
//   0xc0000072  account disabled
//   0xc000006d  generic logon failure (bad user name or authentication info)

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Lower failThreshold to 5 for administrator accounts; add 'and TargetUserName =~ "administrator"' for a focused hunt.
// Add LogonType 2 and 7 if you want console unlock and workstation guessing as well.
// Enrich IpAddress with geo_info_from_ip_address(IpAddress) and add a threat intelligence join.
// Run on a schedule of 15m over a 1h lookback for a Sentinel analytics rule and entity-map Account and IP.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Join keys Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName keep the join small; summarise failures before joining.
// Put the smaller table on the left side of the join; here the aggregated Failures table is smaller than the Successes set.
// SecurityEvent can be huge: always filter on EventID and TimeGenerated first.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Joining without Computer: a success on server B after guessing against server A is a different story.
// Including machine accounts or '-' source addresses, which flood the result with local noise.
// Using only EventID 4625 for lockout detection: lockouts are 4740.
// Forgetting that RDP behind Network Level Authentication logs LogonType 3, not only 10.

## What it does
JSON batching sends up to 20 individual requests in a single HTTP call. It reduces round trips for dashboards and sync jobs, and it also moves very long $filter strings out of the URL into the body. Each inner request is still evaluated on its own for permissions and throttling.

## Request
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer [access-token]

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "1",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me?$select=displayName,userPrincipalName"
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/messages?$filter=isRead eq false&$select=subject,from,receivedDateTime&$top=5"
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/users?$filter=startsWith(displayName,'A')&$orderby=displayName&$count=true&$top=10&$select=displayName",
      "headers": { "ConsistencyLevel": "eventual" }
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/memberOf/microsoft.graph.group?$select=id,displayName",
      "headers": { "ConsistencyLevel": "eventual" }
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/drive/recent?$top=5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Rules visible in the example: url is relative and starts with a slash, without version or host. Each request needs a unique id. A request with a body must carry a Content-Type header. An OData type cast such as /memberOf/microsoft.graph.group needs ConsistencyLevel: eventual, set in the inner headers. This first batch is fully parallel: no request has dependsOn.

## Response
```json
{
  "responses": [
    { "id": "1", "status": 200, "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" }, "body": { "displayName": "Anna Meyer" } },
    { "id": "2", "status": 200, "body": { "value": [] } },
    { "id": "3", "status": 200, "body": { "@odata.count": 3, "value": [] } },
    { "id": "4", "status": 403, "body": { "error": { "code": "Authorization_RequestDenied" } } },
    { "id": "5", "status": 200, "body": { "value": [] } }
  ]
}
```
Responses can arrive in any order; match them by id. The outer status 200 only means the batch was parseable; check every inner status. Request 4 failed with 403 in this example, and requests 1, 2, 3 and 5 were not affected.

## Dependent requests (fully sequential batch)
Use dependsOn when one request must run after another, for example read after write. The documentation recommends that a batch is either fully sequential or fully parallel, so every request after the first lists its predecessor:
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "1",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "dependsOn": ["1"],
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]?$select=displayName,department"
    }
  ]
}
```
If request 1 fails, request 2 is not executed and returns status 424 (Failed Dependency). The batch cannot pass data from one request to the next; dependsOn only controls the order.

## A write example
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "a",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    },
    {
      "id": "b",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[other-user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to adapt
- Bulk reads by id: GET /users/[id1], /users/[id2] and so on, 20 per call, to resolve many ids quickly.
- Combine requests for different workloads, but remember that the token must carry the permissions of all inner requests.
- Keep a batch fully parallel or fully sequential: the documentation recommends not mixing independent requests with dependsOn chains in one batch.

## Required permissions
There is no permission for the batch endpoint itself. Each inner request requires the permissions of its own resource, for example User.Read for /me and for /me/memberOf (GroupMember.Read.All or Directory.Read.All for broader group access), Mail.Read for /me/messages, Files.Read for /me/drive/recent. A missing permission only fails the affected inner request with 403.

## Common mistakes
- More than 20 requests in one batch, or a duplicate id, which fails the whole batch with 400.
- Absolute URLs or including /v1.0 in the inner url.
- Forgetting Content-Type on inner requests that have a body.
- Treating batch HTTP 200 as success for everything.
- Missing ConsistencyLevel for advanced queries inside the batch; set it in the inner headers.

## Performance and throttling notes
Batching reduces network overhead but not server-side limits: each inner request counts against throttling, and the underlying service (Exchange, SharePoint, directory) applies its own limits. Inner requests that exceed limits return 429 with a Retry-After header in the inner response headers; resend only those requests after the delay, not the whole batch.

# Microsoft 365 license inventory and bulk license assignment from CSV

Two jobs in one script: first, an inventory of every subscribed SKU (purchased, consumed, free); second, an optional bulk assignment driven by a CSV with the columns UserPrincipalName and SkuPartNumber.

## What it does
- Reads subscribed SKUs with Get-MgSubscribedSku and writes an inventory CSV.
- If -InputCsv is given, validates each row, sets a missing usage location, checks that units are free and assigns the license with Set-MgUserLicense.
- Skips users who already hold the SKU and reports whether the license came directly or from a group.
- Honors -WhatIf, so you can preview the whole run; the inventory and the result log are still written during a -WhatIf run.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  License inventory and CSV-driven assignment.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Set-Licenses.ps1                                   # inventory only
  .\Set-Licenses.ps1 -InputCsv .\new-hires.csv -WhatIf # preview assignment
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]
param(
    [string]$InputCsv,
    [ValidatePattern('^[A-Z]{2}$')][string]$DefaultUsageLocation = 'DE',
    [string]$OutputFolder = '.'
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
# Inventory only needs a read scope; request the write scope only when a CSV is assigned
$scopes = @('Organization.Read.All')
if ($InputCsv) { $scopes += 'User.ReadWrite.All' }
try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes $scopes -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

# --- Inventory ---
$skus = Get-MgSubscribedSku -All
$inventory = $skus | ForEach-Object {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        SkuPartNumber = $_.SkuPartNumber
        SkuId         = $_.SkuId
        Purchased     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Enabled
        Consumed      = $_.ConsumedUnits
        Available     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Enabled - $_.ConsumedUnits
        Suspended     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Suspended
        Warning       = $_.PrepaidUnits.Warning
    }
}
$invPath = Join-Path $OutputFolder "LicenseInventory_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
$inventory | Sort-Object SkuPartNumber | Export-Csv $invPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$inventory | Sort-Object SkuPartNumber | Format-Table SkuPartNumber, Purchased, Consumed, Available -AutoSize

if (-not $InputCsv) { return }

# --- Assignment ---
$skuMap = @{}; foreach ($i in $inventory) { $skuMap[$i.SkuPartNumber] = $i }
$rows = Import-Csv $InputCsv
$log = foreach ($row in $rows) {
    $upn = $row.UserPrincipalName.Trim(); $part = $row.SkuPartNumber.Trim()
    $result = 'Assigned'
    try {
        if (-not $skuMap.ContainsKey($part)) { throw "Unknown SKU $part" }
        $sku  = $skuMap[$part]
        $user = Get-MgUser -UserId $upn -Property id,usageLocation,assignedLicenses,licenseAssignmentStates -ErrorAction Stop

        $existing = @($user.LicenseAssignmentStates | Where-Object { $_.SkuId -eq $sku.SkuId })
        if ($existing.Count -gt 0) {
            # a user can hold the same SKU directly and through a group at the same time
            $result = if (@($existing | Where-Object { -not $_.AssignedByGroup }).Count -gt 0) { 'AlreadyAssignedDirect' } else { 'AlreadyAssignedViaGroup' }
        }
        elseif ($sku.Available -le 0) { $result = 'NoLicensesAvailable' }
        else {
            if (-not $user.UsageLocation -and $PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($upn, "Set usage location $DefaultUsageLocation")) {
                Update-MgUser -UserId $user.Id -UsageLocation $DefaultUsageLocation -ErrorAction Stop
            }
            if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($upn, "Assign $part")) {
                Set-MgUserLicense -UserId $user.Id -AddLicenses @(@{ SkuId = $sku.SkuId }) -RemoveLicenses @() -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
                $sku.Available--
            }
            else { $result = 'WhatIf' }
        }
    }
    catch { $result = "Error: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
    [pscustomobject]@{ UserPrincipalName = $upn; Sku = $part; Result = $result }
}
$logPath = Join-Path $OutputFolder "LicenseAssignment_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd_HHmm').csv"
$log | Export-Csv $logPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$log | Group-Object Result | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Delegated scopes: Organization.Read.All (SKUs) and User.ReadWrite.All (assignment).
- Role: License Administrator, User Administrator or Global Administrator.
- Inventory only needs Organization.Read.All and a reader role; the script requests User.ReadWrite.All only when -InputCsv is used. LicenseAssignment.ReadWrite.All is enough for assignment if every user already has a usage location.

## How to adapt
- Use group-based licensing for steady state and keep this script for exceptions, migrations and backfills.
- Remove licenses by passing the SkuId in -RemoveLicenses and an empty array in -AddLicenses.
- Disable individual service plans with @{ SkuId = ...; DisabledPlans = @('plan-guid') } in -AddLicenses.
- Extend the CSV with a DisabledPlans column if you assign trimmed-down suites.

## Common mistakes
- Missing usage location: assignment fails with a "invalid usage location" style error until it is set.
- Assigning directly to users who already receive the license via a group; later removal from the group will not remove the direct license and the cost stays.
- Using the display name of a product instead of SkuPartNumber (for example SPE_E3).
- Ignoring the Available count: assignments beyond purchased units fail or consume trial units unexpectedly.

# Find Microsoft Teams teams with no owner or a single owner

Ownerless teams cannot be managed: nobody approves members, renames channels or archives the team. This script finds them, and also catches the quieter risk of teams whose only owner has left or been disabled.

## What it does
- Lists all Microsoft 365 groups provisioned as Teams through the Graph resourceProvisioningOptions filter.
- Reads owners and member counts for each team.
- Looks up whether each owner account is still enabled.
- Assigns a risk label: NoOwner, OnlyDisabledOwners, SingleOwner or OK.
- Optionally adds a fallback owner (for example an IT governance mailbox user) to NoOwner teams, supporting -WhatIf.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Reports Teams without a healthy owner set.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-TeamsOwnerHealth.ps1
  .\Get-TeamsOwnerHealth.ps1 -FallbackOwnerUpn 'teams-governance@contoso.com' -WhatIf
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess, ConfirmImpact = 'Medium')]
param(
    [string]$FallbackOwnerUpn,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\TeamsOwnerHealth_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$scopes = @('Group.Read.All','User.Read.All')
if ($FallbackOwnerUpn) { $scopes = @('Group.ReadWrite.All','User.Read.All') }

try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes $scopes -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$fallback = $null
if ($FallbackOwnerUpn) {
    try { $fallback = Get-MgUser -UserId $FallbackOwnerUpn -Property id,userPrincipalName }
    catch { Write-Error "Fallback owner not found: $FallbackOwnerUpn"; return }
}

try {
    $teams = Get-MgGroup -Filter "resourceProvisioningOptions/Any(x:x eq 'Team')" -All `
             -Property id,displayName,visibility,createdDateTime,mail,renewedDateTime
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not list teams: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }
Write-Host "Checking $($teams.Count) teams..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

$userCache = @{}
$report = foreach ($t in $teams) {
    try {
        $owners = @(Get-MgGroupOwner -GroupId $t.Id -All -ErrorAction Stop)
        $memberCount = @(Get-MgGroupMember -GroupId $t.Id -All -ErrorAction Stop).Count
    }
    catch { Write-Warning "Skipping $($t.DisplayName): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }

    $enabled = 0; $names = @()
    foreach ($o in $owners) {
        if (-not $userCache.ContainsKey($o.Id)) {
            $userCache[$o.Id] = try { Get-MgUser -UserId $o.Id -Property id,accountEnabled,userPrincipalName -ErrorAction Stop } catch { $null }
        }
        $u = $userCache[$o.Id]
        if ($u) { $names += $u.UserPrincipalName; if ($u.AccountEnabled) { $enabled++ } }
    }

    $risk = if ($owners.Count -eq 0) { 'NoOwner' }
            elseif ($enabled -eq 0)  { 'OnlyDisabledOwners' }
            elseif ($enabled -eq 1)  { 'SingleOwner' }
            else { 'OK' }

    if ($fallback -and $risk -in 'NoOwner','OnlyDisabledOwners') {
        if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($t.DisplayName, "Add owner $FallbackOwnerUpn")) {
            try {
                New-MgGroupOwnerByRef -GroupId $t.Id -BodyParameter @{
                    '@odata.id' = "https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/$($fallback.Id)" } -ErrorAction Stop
                $risk += ' (fallback added)'
            }
            catch { Write-Warning "Owner add failed for $($t.DisplayName): $($_.Exception.Message)" }
        }
    }

    [pscustomobject]@{
        Team = $t.DisplayName; Visibility = $t.Visibility; Created = $t.CreatedDateTime
        OwnerCount = $owners.Count; EnabledOwners = $enabled; Owners = ($names -join ';')
        MemberCount = $memberCount; Risk = $risk; GroupId = $t.Id
    }
}
$report | Sort-Object Risk, Team | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$report | Group-Object { ($_.Risk -split ' ')[0] } | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Report: Group.Read.All and User.Read.All; roles Global Reader or Teams Administrator.
- Fixing owners: Group.ReadWrite.All; roles Groups Administrator, Teams Administrator or Global Administrator.

## How to adapt
- Instead of a fixed fallback owner, promote the longest-standing member by reading group members and calling New-MgGroupOwnerByRef for that user.
- Combine with Get-MgGroup -Property renewedDateTime and expiration policy data to spot teams that will expire soon.
- Send each team's remaining owner a mail via Send-MgUserMail reminding them to add a second owner.
- Add a column for the sensitivity label using the assignedLabels property.

## Common mistakes
- Using Get-MgTeam for the list: it returns fewer properties and does not expose owners the same way. Query groups instead.
- Counting guests as owners: guests cannot be Team owners in some tenant configurations, so check owner userType.
- Adding a mailbox or service account as owner without a license or sign-in plan, so it cannot approve anything.
- Forgetting private channels and shared channels have their own owners, which this script does not inspect.

# SharePoint Online permission audit with PnP PowerShell

This script inventories who has access to a site and to every list or library that breaks permission inheritance, and flags the principals that usually signal oversharing. It is read-only.

## What it does
- Connects to each site you pass with interactive sign-in and your own Entra app registration.
- Reads site-level role assignments and expands SharePoint groups into their members.
- Finds lists and libraries with unique permissions and reports their role assignments as well.
- Flags Everyone, Everyone except external users, and external (#ext#) accounts.
- Writes one flat CSV: Site, Scope, Object, Principal, PrincipalType, PermissionLevels, Flag.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Read-only permission audit for SharePoint Online sites.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-SpPermissionAudit.ps1 -SiteUrl 'https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr' -ClientId '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000'
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string[]]$SiteUrl,
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$ClientId,      # your PnP Entra app registration
    [switch]$SkipLists,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\SpPermissionAudit_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

function Get-Flag([string]$login, [string]$type) {
    if ($login -match 'spo-grid-all-users')       { return 'EveryoneExceptExternal' }
    if ($login -match '^c:0\(\.s\|true$')           { return 'Everyone' }
    if ($login -match '#ext#')                      { return 'ExternalUser' }
    return ''
}

function Get-AssignmentRows($clientObject, [string]$site, [string]$scope, [string]$objectName) {
    $assignments = Get-PnPProperty -ClientObject $clientObject -Property RoleAssignments
    foreach ($ra in $assignments) {
        Get-PnPProperty -ClientObject $ra -Property RoleDefinitionBindings, Member | Out-Null
        $levels = ($ra.RoleDefinitionBindings | ForEach-Object { $_.Name }) -join ';'
        $m = $ra.Member
        if ($m.PrincipalType -eq 'SharePointGroup') {
            $members = @()
            try { $members = @(Get-PnPGroupMember -Group $m.Title -ErrorAction Stop) } catch { }
            if ($members.Count -eq 0) {
                [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal="$($m.Title) (empty group)"
                    PrincipalType='SharePointGroup'; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag='' }
            }
            foreach ($u in $members) {
                [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal="$($u.Title) via $($m.Title)"
                    PrincipalType=$u.PrincipalType; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag=(Get-Flag $u.LoginName $u.PrincipalType) }
            }
        }
        else {
            [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal=$m.Title
                PrincipalType=$m.PrincipalType; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag=(Get-Flag $m.LoginName $m.PrincipalType) }
        }
    }
}

$all = foreach ($url in $SiteUrl) {
    try {
        Connect-PnPOnline -Url $url -Interactive -ClientId $ClientId
        $web = Get-PnPWeb
        Get-AssignmentRows -clientObject $web -site $url -scope 'Site' -objectName $web.Title

        if (-not $SkipLists) {
            $lists = Get-PnPList -Includes HasUniqueRoleAssignments | Where-Object { -not $_.Hidden -and $_.HasUniqueRoleAssignments }
            foreach ($l in $lists) {
                Get-AssignmentRows -clientObject $l -site $url -scope 'List' -objectName $l.Title
            }
        }
    }
    catch { Write-Warning "Failed for ${url}: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
}

$all | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8
Write-Host "Rows: $(@($all).Count). Flagged: $(@($all | Where-Object Flag).Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$all | Where-Object Flag | Format-Table Site, Object, Principal, Flag, PermissionLevels -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Run as a Site Collection Administrator (or SharePoint Administrator) of every audited site; reading role assignments needs Manage Permissions style access.
- PnP.PowerShell needs an Entra app registration (-ClientId) with delegated SharePoint permissions and consent; since 2024 PnP no longer ships a shared multi-tenant app.
- For tenant-wide runs, enumerate sites first with Connect-PnPOnline to the admin URL and Get-PnPTenantSite.

## How to adapt
- Add item-level checks for sensitive libraries with Get-PnPListItem and HasUniqueRoleAssignments, but expect long runtimes.
- Pair with sharing link reports (Get-PnPFileSharingLink or the SharePoint Advanced Management data access governance reports) for Anyone and company-wide links.
- Replace the flag logic with a list of your own sensitive security groups to find "broad group on confidential site" cases.
- Schedule it against Hub site members and diff the CSV against last month.

## Common mistakes
- Auditing only site-level access: most leaks come from broken inheritance on libraries and folders.
- Treating empty SharePoint groups as harmless; they are often the leftover of an unfinished cleanup.
- Forgetting that Microsoft 365 group members are not listed as SharePoint group members, so the Members group shows a single "group" principal that must be expanded in Entra.
- Running without an own -ClientId and wondering why sign-in fails.

# Azure tag compliance report across subscriptions with optional remediation

Tags drive cost allocation, ownership and automation. This script reports which resources and resource groups lack the tags your governance standard requires, and can optionally merge default values.

## What it does
- Iterates over the subscriptions you name (or all enabled subscriptions you can see).
- Checks every resource, and optionally resource groups, for required tag names (case-insensitive, like Azure) and empty values.
- Exports a CSV with the missing tags per resource.
- With -ApplyDefaults it merges default values for missing tags using Update-AzTag, honoring -WhatIf.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Tag compliance report for Azure resources.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-AzTagCompliance.ps1 -RequiredTags CostCenter,Owner,Environment -IncludeResourceGroups
  .\Get-AzTagCompliance.ps1 -RequiredTags Environment -DefaultTagValues @{Environment='unassigned'} -ApplyDefaults -WhatIf
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess, ConfirmImpact = 'Medium')]
param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string[]]$RequiredTags,
    [string[]]$SubscriptionId,
    [hashtable]$DefaultTagValues = @{},
    [switch]$IncludeResourceGroups,
    [switch]$ApplyDefaults,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\AzTagCompliance_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

# Sign in only if there is no active context (no secrets in the script)
if (-not (Get-AzContext)) {
    # sign-in only reads; run it with WhatIf off so a -WhatIf run does not skip it
    try { & { $WhatIfPreference = $false; Connect-AzAccount -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } }
    catch { Write-Error "Azure sign-in failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }
}

try {
    $subs = if ($SubscriptionId) { $SubscriptionId | ForEach-Object { Get-AzSubscription -SubscriptionId $_ } }
            else { Get-AzSubscription | Where-Object State -eq 'Enabled' }
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not list subscriptions: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

function Get-MissingTags($tags, [string[]]$required) {
    foreach ($name in $required) {
        $key = if ($tags) { $tags.Keys | Where-Object { $_ -ieq $name } | Select-Object -First 1 }
        if (-not $key -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$tags[$key])) { $name }
    }
}

$report = foreach ($sub in $subs) {
    Write-Host "Subscription: $($sub.Name)" -ForegroundColor Cyan
    # Set-AzContext honors -WhatIf; without this, a -WhatIf run would keep scanning the first subscription
    try { & { $WhatIfPreference = $false; Set-AzContext -SubscriptionId $sub.Id -TenantId $sub.TenantId -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } }
    catch { Write-Warning "Skipping $($sub.Name): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }

    try { $resources = @(Get-AzResource -ErrorAction Stop) }
    catch { Write-Warning "Could not list resources in $($sub.Name): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }
    $targets = @($resources |
        ForEach-Object { [pscustomobject]@{ Type=$_.ResourceType; Name=$_.Name; Rg=$_.ResourceGroupName; Id=$_.ResourceId; Tags=$_.Tags } })
    if ($IncludeResourceGroups) {
        $targets += @(Get-AzResourceGroup | ForEach-Object {
            [pscustomobject]@{ Type='resourceGroup'; Name=$_.ResourceGroupName; Rg=$_.ResourceGroupName; Id=$_.ResourceId; Tags=$_.Tags } })
    }

    foreach ($t in $targets) {
        $missing = @(Get-MissingTags $t.Tags $RequiredTags)
        if ($missing.Count -eq 0) { continue }
        $action = ''
        if ($ApplyDefaults) {
            $merge = @{}
            foreach ($m in $missing) { if ($DefaultTagValues.ContainsKey($m)) { $merge[$m] = $DefaultTagValues[$m] } }
            if ($merge.Count -gt 0 -and $PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($t.Id, "Merge tags: $($merge.Keys -join ',')")) {
                try { Update-AzTag -ResourceId $t.Id -Tag $merge -Operation Merge -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null; $action = 'DefaultsApplied' }
                catch { $action = "Failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
            }
        }
        [pscustomobject]@{
            Subscription = $sub.Name; ResourceGroup = $t.Rg; Type = $t.Type; Name = $t.Name
            MissingTags = ($missing -join ';'); Action = $action; ResourceId = $t.Id
        }
    }
}
$report | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
Write-Host "Non-compliant objects: $(@($report).Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$report | Group-Object Subscription | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Reader on the subscriptions for the report.
- Tag Contributor (or Contributor) on the scope for -ApplyDefaults.
- Modules: Az.Accounts and Az.Resources.

## How to adapt
- For very large estates use Azure Resource Graph (Search-AzGraph from Az.ResourceGraph) instead of Get-AzResource; it is much faster.
- Enforce the standard permanently with Azure Policy (for example "Require a tag on resources" and "Inherit a tag from the resource group" with modify effect).
- Look up owners from a CMDB CSV and pass per-resource-group values instead of one static default.
- Run it in Azure Automation with a managed identity that has Reader.

## Common mistakes
- Treating tag names as case-sensitive: Azure tag names are case-insensitive, values are case-sensitive.
- Expecting resource group tags to flow to resources automatically; they do not without policy.
- Applying meaningless defaults such as "unknown" everywhere, which hides the problem instead of fixing it.
- Forgetting that some resource types do not support tags, so they appear as permanent exceptions.
- Expecting -WhatIf to switch everything off: cmdlets such as Set-AzContext, Connect-AzAccount and Export-Csv inherit it and would be skipped. The script suppresses WhatIf for these read-only steps and applies it only to the tag changes.

# Exchange Online mailbox forwarding and inbox rule audit

Attackers who compromise a mailbox often add a hidden inbox rule that forwards mail outside. Users also forward to private accounts for convenience. This script checks all three places where forwarding hides.

## What it does
- Reads ForwardingSmtpAddress, ForwardingAddress and DeliverToMailboxAndForward from all mailboxes and resolves ForwardingAddress recipients (mailboxes or mail contacts) to their primary SMTP address.
- Reads inbox rules (including hidden ones) with ForwardTo, ForwardAsAttachmentTo or RedirectTo.
- Lists transport rules that redirect or blind-copy mail.
- Extracts SMTP addresses and marks them External when the domain is not one of your accepted domains.
- Shows the AutoForwardingMode of the outbound spam policies, which decides whether external forwarding is actually blocked.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Audits mailbox forwarding, inbox rules and transport rules.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-ForwardingAudit.ps1 -OutputFolder C:\Temp
  .\Get-ForwardingAudit.ps1 -SkipInboxRules          # fast mode, settings only
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [switch]$SkipInboxRules,
    [string]$OutputFolder = '.'
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
try { Connect-ExchangeOnline -ShowBanner:$false }
catch { Write-Error "Exchange Online connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$accepted = @(Get-AcceptedDomain | ForEach-Object { $_.DomainName.ToString().ToLower() })
$mailRegex = '[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}'

function Get-Addresses($values) {
    if (-not $values) { return @() }
    [regex]::Matches((@($values) -join ' '), $mailRegex) | ForEach-Object { $_.Value.ToLower() } | Select-Object -Unique
}
function Test-External([string[]]$addresses) {
    foreach ($a in $addresses) { if ($accepted -notcontains ($a -split '@')[-1]) { return $true } }
    return $false
}

$stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd'
$findings = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]

# 1. Mailbox-level forwarding
$mailboxes = Get-EXOMailbox -ResultSize Unlimited -RecipientTypeDetails UserMailbox, SharedMailbox `
    -Properties ForwardingAddress, ForwardingSmtpAddress, DeliverToMailboxAndForward
foreach ($m in $mailboxes) {
    if ($m.ForwardingSmtpAddress -or $m.ForwardingAddress) {
        $targets = @($m.ForwardingSmtpAddress, $m.ForwardingAddress) | Where-Object { $_ }
        $addr = @(Get-Addresses $targets)
        if ($m.ForwardingAddress) {
            # ForwardingAddress holds a recipient (mailbox or mail contact), not an SMTP string: resolve its primary address
            try { $addr += (Get-Recipient -Identity $m.ForwardingAddress -ErrorAction Stop).PrimarySmtpAddress.ToString().ToLower() }
            catch { Write-Warning "Could not resolve ForwardingAddress of $($m.UserPrincipalName): $($_.Exception.Message)" }
        }
        $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox=$m.UserPrincipalName; Source='MailboxForwarding'; Name='';
            Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=$m.DeliverToMailboxAndForward
            External=[bool](Test-External $addr); Enabled=$true })
    }
}

# 2. Inbox rules (slow on large tenants)
if (-not $SkipInboxRules) {
    $i = 0
    foreach ($m in $mailboxes) {
        $i++; if ($i % 100 -eq 0) { Write-Progress -Activity 'Inbox rules' -Status "$i of $($mailboxes.Count)" -PercentComplete (100 * $i / $mailboxes.Count) }
        try { $rules = Get-InboxRule -Mailbox $m.UserPrincipalName -IncludeHidden -ErrorAction Stop }
        catch { Write-Warning "Rules failed for $($m.UserPrincipalName): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }
        foreach ($r in $rules | Where-Object { $_.ForwardTo -or $_.ForwardAsAttachmentTo -or $_.RedirectTo }) {
            $targets = @($r.ForwardTo) + @($r.ForwardAsAttachmentTo) + @($r.RedirectTo) | Where-Object { $_ }
            $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox=$m.UserPrincipalName; Source='InboxRule'; Name=$r.Name
                Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=''; External=[bool](Test-External (Get-Addresses $targets)); Enabled=$r.Enabled })
        }
    }
}

# 3. Transport rules
foreach ($t in Get-TransportRule | Where-Object { $_.RedirectMessageTo -or $_.BlindCopyTo -or $_.CopyTo }) {
    $targets = @($t.RedirectMessageTo) + @($t.BlindCopyTo) + @($t.CopyTo) | Where-Object { $_ }
    $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox='(organization)'; Source='TransportRule'; Name=$t.Name
        Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=''; External=[bool](Test-External (Get-Addresses $targets)); Enabled=($t.State -eq 'Enabled') })
}

$path = Join-Path $OutputFolder "ForwardingAudit_$stamp.csv"
$findings | Sort-Object External -Descending | Export-Csv $path -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8
Write-Host "Findings: $($findings.Count), external: $(@($findings | Where-Object External).Count). Output: $path" -ForegroundColor Green

Get-HostedOutboundSpamFilterPolicy | Select-Object Name, AutoForwardingMode | Format-Table -AutoSize
Disconnect-ExchangeOnline -Confirm:$false
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Exchange Online roles: a read-only role group such as View-Only Organization Management, or Global Reader, covers the recipient and configuration cmdlets used here. Check Get-InboxRule and Get-TransportRule in the Exchange permissions reference for your role group; if one is denied, add the role it names.
- Module: ExchangeOnlineManagement 3.x. Sign-in is interactive with modern authentication.

## How to adapt
- Remediate with Set-Mailbox -Identity -ForwardingSmtpAddress $null -ForwardingAddress $null and Remove-InboxRule, wrapped in ShouldProcess and a ticket reference.
- Block external auto-forwarding tenant-wide with Set-HostedOutboundSpamFilterPolicy -AutoForwardingMode Off after you review legitimate cases.
- Add Search-UnifiedAuditLog for New-InboxRule and Set-InboxRule events to find who created suspicious rules and when.
- Run weekly and alert when a new External finding appears.

## Common mistakes
- Checking only mailbox forwarding: most malicious forwarding is done through inbox rules.
- Forgetting -IncludeHidden, which is where attackers hide rules from the Outlook UI.
- Assuming forwarding to an internal contact object is safe; check whether that contact points to an external address.
- Disabling forwarding without telling the user first, which breaks legitimate business workflows.

# Entra app registration secret and certificate expiry report

Expired client secrets cause silent outages: a flow stops, an integration fails, a deployment pipeline breaks. This script finds credentials that have expired or will expire soon, and names the owners to contact.

## What it does
- Reads all application registrations with their password credentials (client secrets) and key credentials (certificates).
- Calculates days until expiry and classifies each credential as Expired, ExpiringSoon or OK.
- Resolves application owners so the report can be routed to a person.
- Optionally includes service principals, which covers SAML signing certificates of enterprise applications.
- Writes a CSV sorted by urgency.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Reports expiring and expired app registration credentials.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-AppCredentialExpiry.ps1 -WarnDays 45 -IncludeEnterpriseApps
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [ValidateRange(1,730)][int]$WarnDays = 30,
    [switch]$IncludeEnterpriseApps,
    [switch]$ExpiringOnly,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\AppCredentialExpiry_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes 'Application.Read.All' -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$now = Get-Date
function Get-Status([datetime]$end) {
    if ($end -lt $now) { 'Expired' }
    elseif ($end -lt $now.AddDays($WarnDays)) { 'ExpiringSoon' }
    else { 'OK' }
}

$ownerCache = @{}
function Get-OwnerList($objectId, [string]$kind) {
    if ($ownerCache.ContainsKey($objectId)) { return $ownerCache[$objectId] }
    try {
        $owners = if ($kind -eq 'App') { Get-MgApplicationOwner -ApplicationId $objectId -All -ErrorAction Stop }
                  else { Get-MgServicePrincipalOwner -ServicePrincipalId $objectId -All -ErrorAction Stop }
        $text = ($owners | ForEach-Object { $_.AdditionalProperties['userPrincipalName'] } | Where-Object { $_ }) -join ';'
    }
    catch { $text = '(owner lookup failed)' }
    $ownerCache[$objectId] = $text   # one lookup per object, not per credential
    return $text
}

function New-Row($obj, [string]$kind, $cred, [string]$credType) {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        ObjectType    = $kind
        DisplayName   = $obj.DisplayName
        AppId         = $obj.AppId
        CredentialType= $credType
        CredentialName= $cred.DisplayName
        KeyId         = $cred.KeyId
        Start         = $cred.StartDateTime
        End           = $cred.EndDateTime
        DaysLeft      = [int]($cred.EndDateTime - $now).TotalDays
        Status        = Get-Status $cred.EndDateTime
        Owners        = Get-OwnerList $obj.Id $kind
        ObjectId      = $obj.Id
    }
}

try {
    $apps = Get-MgApplication -All -PageSize 500 -Property id,appId,displayName,passwordCredentials,keyCredentials
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not read applications: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$rows = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
foreach ($a in $apps) {
    foreach ($c in $a.PasswordCredentials) { $rows.Add((New-Row $a 'App' $c 'Secret')) }
    foreach ($c in $a.KeyCredentials)      { $rows.Add((New-Row $a 'App' $c 'Certificate')) }
}

if ($IncludeEnterpriseApps) {
    $sps = Get-MgServicePrincipal -All -PageSize 500 -Property id,appId,displayName,passwordCredentials,keyCredentials
    foreach ($s in $sps) {
        foreach ($c in $s.PasswordCredentials) { $rows.Add((New-Row $s 'ServicePrincipal' $c 'Secret')) }
        foreach ($c in $s.KeyCredentials)      { $rows.Add((New-Row $s 'ServicePrincipal' $c 'Certificate')) }
    }
}

$result = $rows
if ($ExpiringOnly) { $result = $rows | Where-Object { $_.Status -ne 'OK' } }
$result | Sort-Object DaysLeft | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8

Write-Host "Credentials checked: $($rows.Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$rows | Group-Object Status | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
$rows | Where-Object { $_.Status -ne 'OK' } | Sort-Object DaysLeft |
    Select-Object -First 20 DisplayName, CredentialType, End, DaysLeft, Owners | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Delegated scope Application.Read.All with admin consent.
- Roles: Application Administrator, Cloud Application Administrator, Global Reader or Security Reader.

## How to adapt
- Automate the alert: run it in Azure Automation with a managed identity (Application.Read.All application permission) and send the ExpiringSoon rows to Teams or mail.
- Rotate with Add-MgApplicationPassword and Remove-MgApplicationPassword, storing the new secret in Key Vault and never in the script.
- Prefer certificates or federated credentials (workload identity federation) over secrets to remove expiry from the picture.
- Filter by owner to send each person only their own applications.

## Common mistakes
- Reading Get-MgApplicationOwner output as full user objects: UPN sits in AdditionalProperties.
- Using the AppId where Graph expects the object Id (-ApplicationId takes the object Id).
- Ignoring apps without owners. Those are the ones nobody will renew, so assign a team owner.
- Removing the old secret before the consuming system has switched to the new one.

An approval card should change after someone decides, otherwise a second approver can click again. Universal Actions (Action.Execute, schema 1.4 and later) send a verb and the inputs to your bot, which replies with a replacement card. The card below is plain schema 1.5 JSON.

## Card JSON

```json
