## How to use

1. Generate the rows from your monitoring data. Each row is a Container with a ColumnSet of three columns: dot, name and detail, state label.
2. Map your states to the TextBlock color names: Good (green), Warning (yellow), Attention (red), Accent, Dark, Light and Default. Do not use hex colors; Adaptive Cards do not support them and the named colors adapt to dark mode.
3. Post it to a channel on a schedule with a Workflows or Power Automate flow. A trigger on alert state changes keeps the channel quiet when all is well.
4. To update in place, keep the message id and replace the card each time; otherwise people scroll through a history of outdated boards.
5. Viva Connections dashboards can use the same JSON for an Adaptive Card Extension quick view with small adjustments.

## Common mistakes

- Communicating state only by color. The state label (Healthy, Degraded, Outage) is part of every row for this reason.
- Using images for status icons that load from external hosts. Blocked images leave empty gaps; the colored bullet is plain text.
- Forgetting wrap. Long detail lines are cut off in a narrow client; wrap true is set on every TextBlock.
- Putting 30 services into one card. Show only unhealthy ones plus a summary line, and link to the full status page.
- Stating "updated" times without timezone. The card uses UTC explicitly.

## Compatibility notes

- bleed on a Container and container style need 1.2; RichTextBlock and TextRun need 1.2. Everything else works from 1.0.
- msteams width Full lets the card use the whole message width; Outlook and Copilot Studio ignore the property.
- verticalContentAlignment on Column requires schema 1.1 or higher.
- A card should stay below roughly 28 KB in Teams; with this row template that is around 150 rows, far above what you should show.

How to expose a few operations of a CMMS, ERP or MES REST API to a Copilot Studio agent as tools without writing a connector by hand: upload an OpenAPI file, pick the endpoints, describe them well, authenticate and add them to the agent. The page behind the link (Microsoft Learn: extend your agent with tools from a REST API) was opened on 20 November 2026 and is marked prerelease documentation, so screens and limits can change. The OpenAPI file below is an invented example of a small work order API. Use it to practise, then replace it with the specification of your own system.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Maker credentials and a Copilot Studio license, as the page states.
- A copy of the OpenAPI specification and the authentication details of the API.
- REST API tools are created from an OpenAPI v2 specification (JSON). The page says a v3 file is translated to v2 automatically; check the result after the import.
- The page does not state limits for file size or number of operations. Keep the file small and remove endpoints you do not want to expose.
- The feature is in preview. Do not build a production dependency without checking its current status.
- Tools run in the context of a user connection; a data policy for connectors can block them.

=== Template: OpenAPI v2 file (example, fictional host) ===
{
 "swagger": "2.0",
 "info": {
  "title": "Work Order API",
  "description": "Example CMMS work order API used to demonstrate a REST API tool.",
  "version": "1.0.0"
 },
 "host": "cmms.example.com",
 "basePath": "/api",
 "schemes": [
  "https"
 ],
 "paths": {
  "/workorders": {
   "get": {
    "summary": "Find work orders",
    "description": "Finds work orders for an asset, optionally filtered by status.",
    "operationId": "FindWorkOrders",
    "produces": [
     "application/json"
    ],
    "parameters": [
     {
      "name": "assetId",
      "in": "query",
      "required": true,
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Asset or machine ID, for example PRESS-12."
     },
     {
      "name": "status",
      "in": "query",
      "required": false,
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Open, InProgress or Closed."
     },
     {
      "name": "top",
      "in": "query",
      "required": false,
      "type": "integer",
      "description": "Maximum number of results, default 10."
     }
    ],
    "responses": {
     "200": {
      "description": "List of work orders",
      "schema": {
       "type": "array",
       "items": {
        "$ref": "#/definitions/WorkOrder"
       }
      }
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "/workorders/{id}": {
   "get": {
    "summary": "Get one work order",
    "description": "Returns one work order by its number.",
    "operationId": "GetWorkOrder",
    "produces": [
